# MAGIC <details>
# MAGIC
# MAGIC _Speaker notes:_
# MAGIC
# MAGIC - Let's take a look at a high-level CI/CD journey to production.
# MAGIC - To start, you'll want to have a version control strategy in place. For example, your strategy might include dev, stage, and main branches.
# MAGIC - From here, begin by deploying your Databricks project to the development environment. This allows you to test any changes or updates made to your project on development data, using specific development configurations.
# MAGIC - After completing development and creating a pull request into your stage branch, you will then deploy to the staging environment. This ensures testing of your project continues on new staging data with the appropriate staging configurations.
# MAGIC - Once both your development and staging environments have completed testing and passed all tests, you can deploy to your main or production branch. This will fully deploy your project to production, utilizing production data and the necessary production configurations.
# MAGIC - For development, we'll configure the environment to use single-node compute, as our data is small and doesn't require a large cluster. We'll run the project using our user account on the development data.
# MAGIC - In staging, we aim to closely resemble the production environment. Here, we'll use Serverless compute to scale our resources as needed. We'll run the project using a service principal identity, which gives automated tools access to only the necessary Databricks resources, offering better security than using user or group access. This will be done with our staging data.
# MAGIC - Lastly, for production, we'll continue using Serverless compute and the service principal identity. The project will run using production data, and we'll set the project to run weekly to update the data for our consumers.
# MAGIC
# MAGIC </details>

